ets at roughly 29% for Democrats align with a mid-single-digit Republican margin with meaningful uncertainty.", "watch": "A nonpartisan poll or strong debate performance confirming the Democratic internal polling would move this toward a genuine tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Nancy Mace (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Nancy Mace's retirement leaves this Charleston-area seat open, removing the usual incumbent edge but leaving a district that still leans Republican by about six points. Republican Jenny Costa Honeycutt won a contested primary and carries the advantage of the district's partisan baseline, though she has raised far less than her opponent and holds limited cash. Democrat Nancy Lacore, a former Navy official, has outraised her several times over, earned a spot in the DCCC's Red to Blue program, and is advertising on healthcare costs.\n\nRaters overwhelmingly call the race Likely Republican, with one at Lean R and one at Tossup. Democratic-sponsored polls show a narrow contest, while prediction markets price Democratic chances near three in ten. The forecast lands on a Republican margin of about five points, with enough uncertainty that a strong Democratic finish remains plausible.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2812, "margin": -3.39}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2317, "margin": -5.0}]}